## Appendix A A: model Hamiltonian

Here, we present detailed explanation for the model Hamiltonian. In CeB\({}_{6}\), the conduction band is composed of \(5d\) electrons on Ce-ions, Here, to simplify the model Hamiltonian, we introduce the conduction band made of \(s\) electrons. The realistic tight-binding model of conduction band of CeB\({}_{6}\) is given in Ref. [1]. In the present study, we slightly modify the model in Ref. [1] and put \(k_{z}=0\), in order to reproduce the experimental Fermi surfaces of CeB\({}_{6}\) on the \(k_{x}\)-\(k_{y}\) plane after \(s\)-\(f\) hybridization. The present two-dimensional tight-binding model for conduction band is given as

\[\epsilon_{\mathbf{k}} = t_{ss}^{1}\left(\cos k_{x}+\cos k_{y}\right)\] (S1) \[+ t_{ss}^{2}\left\{\cos(k_{x}+k_{y})+\cos(k_{x}-k_{y})\right\}\] \[+ t_{ss}^{3}\left(\cos 2k_{x}+\cos 2k_{y}\right)\] \[+ \left.t_{ss}^{4}\left\{\cos(2k_{x}+k_{y})+\cos(2k_{x}-k_{y})\right.\right.\] \[+ \left.\left.\cos(2k_{y}+k_{x})+\cos(2k_{y}-k_{x})\right\}\right.\] \[+ \left.t_{ss}^{5}\left\{\cos(2k_{x}+2k_{y})+\cos(2k_{x}-2k_{y})\right\}\right.\] \[+ \left.E_{0},\right.\]

where \(t_{ss}^{i}\) is the \(i\)-th nearest \(s\)-\(s\) hopping integral. We set \((t_{ss}^{1},t_{ss}^{2},t_{ss}^{3},t_{ss}^{4},t_{ss}^{5})=(-0.5,-0.889,0.292,-0.2 29,0.687)\), and \(E_{0}=1.33\).

Next, we explain the hybridization term. Based on the Slater-Koster tight-binding method, the \(s\)-\(f\) hybridization between the nearest Ce-sites is

\[V_{\mathbf{k}f_{1}\uparrow} = -A_{1}t_{sf}(\sin k_{y}-i\sin k_{x}),\] \[V_{\mathbf{k}f_{2}\uparrow} = -A_{2}t_{sf}(\sin k_{y}+i\sin k_{x}),\] (S2)

and \(V_{\mathbf{k}f_{i}\downarrow}=-V_{\mathbf{k}f_{i}\uparrow}^{*}\). Here, \(t_{sf}=(sf\sigma)\), and \(A_{1}=\sqrt{18/14}\) and \(A_{2}=\sqrt{3/7}\). Since \(A_{1}>A_{2}\), the relation \(D_{f_{1}}(0)>D_{f_{2}}(0)\) holds in the present two-dimensional PAM, where \(D_{f_{1}}(0)\) is the \(f_{1}\)-electron density-of-states at Fermi level. However, \(D_{f_{1}}(0)=D_{f_{2}}(0)\) holds in the cubic model, since the \(s\)-\(f\) hybridization along \(z\)-axis is larger for \(f_{2}\)-electron. To escape from the artifact of two-dimensionality, we put \(A_{1}=A_{2}=\sqrt{18/14}\) in the present study.

In the present \(\Gamma_{8}\) model, the relation \(V_{\mathbf{k}f_{1}\sigma}\propto V_{\mathbf{k}f_{2}\sigma}^{*}\) holds as shown in Eq. (S2). In contrast, in the \(\Gamma_{7}^{(1)}\)-\(\Gamma_{7}^{(2)}\) model for CeCu\({}_{2}\)Si\({}_{2}\) used in Ref. [2], the relation \(V_{\mathbf{k}f_{1}\sigma}\propto V_{\mathbf{k}f_{2}\sigma}\) holds.

Finally, we explain the Coulomb interaction in \(f\)-electrons, which is derived from Slater-Condon parameter \(F^{p}\)[2]. We set \((F^{0},F^{2},F^{4},F^{6})=(5.3,9.09,6.927,4.756)\) in unit eV by referring Ref.[3]. The derived Coulomb interaction is about 6eV. If we use the such large Coulomb interaction in the RPA, the magnetic order appears since the self-energy is dropped in the RPA. Therefore, we introduce the following Coulomb interaction term:

\[\hat{H}_{U} =u\hat{H}_{U}^{0},\] (S3) \[\hat{H}_{U}^{0} =\frac{1}{4}\sum_{LL^{\prime}MM^{\prime}}U_{L,L^{\prime};M,M^{ \prime}}^{0}f_{L}^{\dagger}f_{L^{\prime}}f_{M}f_{M^{\prime}}^{\dagger},\] (S4)

where \(L=(l,\sigma)\) and \(M=(m,\rho)\). \(u\) is the interaction model parameter, and \(\hat{U}^{0}\) is the normalized Coulomb interaction introduced in Ref. [2]. That is, the maximum element of \(\hat{U}^{0}\) is normalized to unity.

### B: multipole-operator

Here, we list the pseudo-spin representation of the multipole operators in TABLE 1, which was first introduced in Ref.[4]. An even-rank (odd-rank) operator corresponds to an electric (magnetic) multipole operator. Each multipole operator of rank \(k\) are composed of \(4\times 4\) tensor \(J_{q}^{(k)}(q=-k\sim k)\)[4; 5] which is given by \([J_{\pm},J_{q}^{(k)}]=\sqrt{(k\mp q)(k\pm q+1)}J_{q\pm 1}^{(k)}\ J_{k}^{(k)}=(-1)^{k} \sqrt{(2k-1)!!/(2k)!!}J_{+}^{k}\). The multipole operators \(\hat{Q}\) is given by the linear combination of \(J_{q}^{(k)}\). The \(4\times 4\) matrix form of each electric (odd-rank) multipole operators is given by [4]

\[\Gamma_{1}^{+} \begin{cases}\hat{1}&=\hat{\sigma}^{0}\hat{\tau}^{0}\\ \hat{O}_{20}&=4.0\hat{\sigma}^{0}\hat{\tau}^{z}\end{cases}\] \[\Gamma_{3}^{+} \begin{cases}\hat{O}_{22}&=4.0\hat{\sigma}^{0}\hat{\tau}^{x}\\ \hat{\sigma}_{xy}&=-\hat{\sigma}^{z}\hat{\tau}^{y}\end{cases}\] \[\Gamma_{5}^{+} \begin{cases}\hat{O}_{yz}&=-\hat{\sigma}^{x}\hat{\tau}^{y}\\ \hat{O}_{zx}&=-\hat{\sigma}^{y}\hat{\tau}^{y}\end{cases}\]

The \(4\times 4\) matrix form of each magnetic (odd-rank) multipole operators is given by [4]

\[\Gamma_{2}^{-} \begin{cases}\hat{J}^{z}&=\hat{\sigma}^{z}\left(-1.2\hat{\tau}^{0}- 0.67\hat{\tau}^{z}\right)\\ \hat{T}^{z\alpha}&=\hat{\sigma}^{z}\left(-1.0\hat{\tau}^{0}-7.0\hat{\tau}^{z} \right)\end{cases}\]\[\Gamma_{3}^{-} \left\{\hat{T}^{xyz} =-10.0\hat{\sigma}^{0}\hat{\tau}^{y}\right.\] \[\Gamma_{4}^{-} \left\{\hat{T}^{z\beta} =-6.7\hat{\sigma}^{z}\hat{\tau}^{x}\right.\] \[\Gamma_{5}^{-} \left\{\begin{matrix}\hat{J}^{x}&=\hat{\sigma}^{x}\left(1.2\hat{ \tau}^{0}-0.34\hat{\tau}^{z}+0.58\hat{\tau}^{x}\right)\\ \hat{J}^{y}&=\hat{\sigma}^{y}\left(1.2\hat{\tau}^{0}-0.34\hat{\tau}^{z}-0.58 \hat{\tau}^{x}\right)\\ \hat{T}^{x\alpha}&=\hat{\sigma}^{x}\left(\hat{\tau}^{0}-3.5\hat{\tau}^{z}+6.1 \hat{\tau}^{x}\right)\\ \hat{T}^{y\alpha}&=\hat{\sigma}^{y}\left(\hat{\tau}^{0}+3.5\hat{\tau}^{z}+6.1 \hat{\tau}^{x}\right)\\ \hat{T}^{x\beta}&=\hat{\sigma}^{x}\left(-5.8\hat{\tau}^{z}-3.4\hat{\tau}^{x} \right)\\ \hat{T}^{y\beta}&=\hat{\sigma}^{y}\left(-5.8\hat{\tau}^{z}+3.4\hat{\tau}^{x} \right)\end{matrix}\] (S6)

In the main text, we use the normalized multipole matrix introduced as follows:

\[\hat{Q}/\sqrt{\sum_{L,M}|Q_{L,M}|^{2}}\rightarrow\hat{Q}.\] (S7)

Then, the normalized \(\hat{Q}\) satisfies the condition \(\sum_{L,M}|Q_{L,M}|^{2}=1\).

### C: multipole fluctuations

In the main text, we explain the analytic expression only for AL1 term. The expression for the AL2 term is given as

\[X^{\text{AL2}}_{\alpha\beta}(q) =\tfrac{T}{2}\sum_{\alpha^{\prime}\beta^{\prime}\alpha^{\prime \prime}\beta^{\prime\prime}}\Lambda^{\alpha}_{\alpha^{\prime}\beta^{\prime \prime}}(q,p)V_{\beta^{\prime\prime}\beta^{\prime}}(p-q)\] (S8) \[\qquad\times V_{\alpha^{\prime\prime}\alpha^{\prime}}(p)\tilde{ \Lambda}^{\beta}_{\alpha^{\prime\prime}\beta^{\prime}}(q,p),\]

where

\[\Lambda^{EF}_{ABCD}(q,p)\equiv-T\sum_{k}G^{f}_{BF}(k-q)G^{f}_{ED}( k)G^{f}_{CA}(k-q+p),\] \[\tilde{\Lambda}^{EF}_{ABCD}(q,p)\equiv-T\sum_{k}G^{f}_{AE}(k+q)G^{ f}_{FC}(k)G^{f}_{DB}(k+q-p).\]

The expression for the MT term is

\[X^{MT}_{LL^{\prime}MM^{\prime}}(q)=T^{2}\sum_{p,k,A\sim D}G_{LA}( k+q-p)G_{BL^{\prime}}(k-p)\] \[\times G_{DM}(k+q)G_{M^{\prime}C}(k)V_{DACB}(p).\] (S9)

The total VC is given by \(\hat{X}^{\text{AL+MT}}=\hat{X}^{AL1}+\hat{X}^{AL2}+\hat{X}^{MT}\), by subtracting the double counting second order diagrams of order \(u^{2}\).

In the main text, we perform the numerical study of multipole susceptibilities by considering both MT- and AL-VCs, and showed that \(O_{xy}\) octupole susceptibility is strongly enlarged by the AL-VCs. Here, we show all the quadrupole susceptibilities obtained by the present study in Fig. S1. In the cubic model, \(\chi^{Q}(\mathbf{q},0)\) with \(Q=O_{xy},O_{zx},O_{yz}\) should equally develop. In the present two-dimensional model, however, only \(O_{xy}\)-fluctuation strongly develops. The reason is that \((T_{x},T_{y})\) fluctuations are much larger than \(T_{z}\) fluctuations in the RPA, due to the violation of cubic symmetry. Since \(O_{\mu\nu}\) quadrupole susceptibility is magnified by \((T_{\mu},T_{\nu})\) fluctuations \((\mu,\nu=x,y,z)\) due to the AL-VC, \(\chi^{O_{xy}}(\mathbf{q},0)\) is the largest in the present model.

As we show in TABLE 2, the Coulomb interaction \(U_{0}^{Q}\) for \(Q=O_{xy/yz/xz}\) is much larger than that for \(Q=O_{20/22}\). For this reason, it is difficult to expect that \(Q=O_{20/22}\) quadrupole susceptibility becomes larger than \(Q=O_{xy}\) one, even if the AL-VCs are considered. Thus, the relation \(\chi^{O_{xy}}(\mathbf{q},0)>\chi^{O_{20/22}}(\mathbf{q},0)\) should hold even in cubic systems.

Next, we calculate the susceptibility with AL-VC (MT-VC), \(\chi^{O_{xy}}_{\text{AL(MT)}}(\mathbf{q},0)\), given by \(\hat{\phi}(q)=\hat{\chi}^{0}(q)+\hat{X}^{\text{AL(MT)}}(q)\). Figure S2 shows the obtained \(\chi^{O_{xy}}_{\text{AL}}(\mathbf{q},0)\) and \(\chi^{O_{xy}}_{\text{MT}}(\mathbf{q},0)\) as functions of \(u\). \(\chi^{O_{xy}}_{\text{AL}}(\mathbf{q},0)\) strongly increases with \(u\), similarly to \(\chi^{O_{xy}}(\mathbf{q},0)\) with AL+MT terms shown in Fig. 3 (c) in the main text. In contrast, \(\chi^{O_{xy}}_{\rm MT}(\mathbf{q},0)\) remains small and comparable to the RPA result in Fig. 3 (c). Therefore, it is verified that the enhancement of \(O_{xy}\) quadrupole fluctuations originates from the AL-VC, whereas the MT-VC is very small.

To understand this result analytically, we analyze the AL and MT terms for the electric multipole channel given by the following magnetic multipole susceptibility

\[\chi^{\rm mag}(\mathbf{q},\omega_{l}) = \frac{a\xi^{2}}{1+\xi^{2}(\mathbf{q}-\mathbf{Q})^{2}+|\omega_{l}|/\omega_{ \rm mag}},\] (S10)

where \(\xi^{2}\propto(T-T_{0})^{-1}\) and \(\omega_{\rm mag}\propto\xi^{-2}\). \(\xi\) is the correlation length. Then, in two-dimensional systems at a fixed \(T\), AL-VC and MT-VC given in Eqs. (S8)-(S9) are scaled as \(X^{\rm AL}(\mathbf{0},0)\sim\sum_{\mathbf{p}}\{\chi^{\rm mag}(\mathbf{p},0)\}^{2}\sim\xi^{2}\) and \(X^{\rm MT}(\mathbf{0},0)\sim\sum_{\mathbf{p}}\chi^{\rm mag}(\mathbf{p},0)\sim\log\xi\), respectively. Therefore, the AL term dominates over the MT term when \(\xi\gg 1\)[6]. The significance of the AL terms near the magnetic criticality is verified by the functional-renormalization-group (fRG) study [7; 8; 9].

In \(d\)-dimensional system, the AL term is proportional to \(\max\{\xi^{4-d},1\}\). This fact means that the non-locality of irreducible AL diagram is significant near the magneitc criticality (\(\xi\gg 1\)).

